@* Card: the white container content sits in on the tinted page.

   Collections go in DtDataTable; everything else goes in one of these. The head is optional because
   a card is sometimes just a surface - the profile screen's three panels each have a title and an
   action, and a summary block on a phone has neither. What is not optional is that the title is an
   <h2>: the screen owns the one <h1>, and a card that titled itself with a <div> would leave a
   reader moving by heading with nothing to move to.

   No coloured side stripe and no nesting. Both are stated in the component's specification rather
   than left to taste, because both are what a screen reaches for when it wants one card to matter
   more than another - and a card that matters more is a card with a title that says so. *@

<div class="dt-card @Class">
    @if (Title is not null)
    {
        <div class="dt-card__head">
            <h2 class="dt-card__title dt-type-h2">@Title</h2>
            @* The actions in a group of their own rather than loose beside the title. Loose,
               every button was a flex child of the head in its own right, so a card offering
               more than one could only lay them out by arguing with the head's own rule - and
               a screen that wanted them stacked had no element to stack. *@
            @if (Actions is not null)
            {
                <div class="dt-card__actions">@Actions</div>
            }
        </div>
    }

    <div class="dt-card__body dt-type-body">
        @ChildContent
    </div>
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// The card's heading, already localized by the screen. Null renders no head at all rather than
    /// an empty bar: a card with nothing to say about itself is a surface, not a titled panel.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? Title { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The actions on the record this card holds, rendered beside the title. Ignored without a
    /// <see cref="Title"/>, which is deliberate: an action with no heading beside it has nothing
    /// naming what it acts on.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// A few, and they wrap: the profile screen's one card carries edit, change password and change
    /// email, which are three ways of changing the one record rather than three cards' worth of
    /// work. What the head will not carry is a row long enough to compete with the title it sits
    /// beside - at which point the extra actions belong in the body with what they act on.
    /// </remarks>
    [Parameter]
    public RenderFragment? Actions { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The card's contents.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public RenderFragment? ChildContent { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The screen's own hook on this card, so a test can name one panel among three. Classes only -
    /// a card is not a place to put a style, and NFR-29 would catch it if it were.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? Class { get; set; }
}
